    <p class="eyebrow">THE SACRED PLACES JOURNAL</p><h1>A river. An island.<br><em>A living heritage.</em></h1>
    <p>Places hold stories in a different way: through rivers, shrines and the communities that care for them. Begin with two centres of pilgrimage and follow their connections into the collection.</p>
    <nav class="journal-index" aria-label="Place directory"><a href="#varanasi">Varanasi · Uttar Pradesh</a><a href="#rameswaram">Rameswaram · Tamil Nadu</a></nav>
  </section>
  <div class="section heritage-dossiers">
    <article class="reader-panel heritage-dossier" id="varanasi">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 / ON THE GANGA</p><h2>Varanasi</h2><figure class="journal-illustration"><img src="/assets/images/varanasi-riverfront-journal.webp" alt="Watercolour interpretation of stepped Ganga ghats, riverside buildings and wooden boats at dawn" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A riverfront inspired by Varanasi · AI-generated illustration. Architecture is interpretive.</figcaption></figure>
      <dl class="journal-facts"><div><dt>Also known as</dt><dd>Kashi and Banaras</dd></div><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>Uttar Pradesh, northern India, on the River Ganga</dd></div><div><dt>A shrine to explore</dt><dd>Shri Kashi Vishwanath, dedicated to Shiva and revered as a jyotirlinga shrine</dd></div></dl>
      <p>Varanasi’s riverfront brings pilgrimage, worship and everyday life into close proximity. Ghats form the city’s meeting places with the Ganga; narrow lanes lead into its temple landscape.</p>
      <h3>Shiva and the river</h3>
      <p>The Kashi Vishwanath shrine offers a connection to Shiva’s devotional world. The Ganga offers another doorway into the stories: in the Ramayana’s account of Bhagiratha, Shiva receives the river’s descent before she follows the king onward.</p>
      <p>This is a reading connection between a sacred river and a narrative in the collection. The Bhagiratha story describes the river’s descent, rather than a historical account of Varanasi.</p>
      <div class="journal-links"><a href="/deities.html#shiva">Explore Shiva</a><a href="/bhagiratha-ganga.html">Read Bhagiratha and the Ganga</a><a href="/festivals.html#shivaratri">Explore Maha Shivaratri</a></div>
      <section class="journal-reading-route" aria-labelledby="kashi-text"><p class="eyebrow">READ THE SACRED LANDSCAPE</p><h3 id="kashi-text">Kashi in the text</h3><p>The Kashi-khanda presents Kashi as the domain of Vishveshvara and associates its sacred landscape with liberation. It connects the name Varanasi with Asi and Varana; a note in the supplied edition identifies this as a popular etymology. Manikarnika and the celestial river also appear in the text’s praise of the city.</p><p>These passages describe a devotional geography. They do not establish the historical origins of present-day buildings or ghats.</p><a href="/sources.html#supplied-journal-books">Source: Kashi-khanda, Purvardha 30.69–81 →</a></section>
      <p class="collection-source"><a href="/sources.html#places">Location sources &amp; sacred context</a></p>
    </article>
    <article class="reader-panel heritage-dossier" id="rameswaram">
      <p class="eyebrow">02 / AN ISLAND OF PILGRIMAGE</p><h2>Rameswaram</h2><figure class="journal-illustration"><img src="/assets/images/rameswaram-corridor-journal.webp" alt="Painted interpretation of a long South Indian temple corridor with carved stone pillars and distant visitors" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A corridor inspired by Rameswaram · AI-generated illustration. Details are interpretive.</figcaption></figure>
      <dl class="journal-facts"><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>Ramanathapuram district, Tamil Nadu, on Rameswaram island</dd></div><div><dt>A shrine to explore</dt><dd>Ramanathaswamy Temple, where Shiva is worshipped as a jyotirlinga</dd></div><div><dt>Story connection</dt><dd>Local traditions of Rama’s worship of Shiva</dd></div></dl>
      <p>The Ramanathaswamy Temple is known for its long corridors and carved pillars. Its sacred setting connects the worship of Shiva with traditions of Rama and the journey to Lanka.</p>
      <h3>A meeting of devotional worlds</h3>
      <p>The district’s official account records the belief that Rama sanctified the place through worship of Shiva, and identifies its importance to both Shaiva and Vaishnava devotees. This is a local sacred tradition, distinct from the ocean-crossing chapter followed in our Hanuman reader.</p>
      <p>Read Hanuman’s journey for the search for Sita, then explore Rameswaram as a place where Ramayana memory and Shiva worship meet. The connection invites comparison without placing every local tradition into the same textual account.</p>
      <div class="journal-links"><a href="/hanuman-lanka.html">Read Hanuman’s journey to Lanka</a><a href="/reading-paths.html#path-3">Enter the Ramayana reading path</a><a href="/deities.html#shiva">Explore Shiva</a></div>
      <section class="journal-reading-route" aria-labelledby="setu-text"><p class="eyebrow">RAMA AND RAMANATHA</p><h3 id="setu-text">One shrine, a distinct textual account</h3><p>The Setu-mahatmya praises the Shiva linga associated with Rama. It describes Rama as its installer and worshipper, and connects Ramanatha’s presence with liberation. A note in the supplied edition explains that Ramanatha and Ramesvara are names for the same linga.</p><p>This belongs to the Skanda Purana’s sacred account of Setu. It remains distinct from the Valmiki Ramayana narrative and from the later architectural history of the temple.</p><a href="/sources.html#supplied-journal-books">Source: Setu-mahatmya 43.1–14a →</a></section>
      <p class="collection-source"><a href="/sources.html#places">Location sources &amp; tradition distinctions</a></p>
    </article>
